"Construct Intelligence": {
  "index": "-95",
  "category": "constructs",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "title",
      "value": "Construct Intelligence"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "json": [
        {
          "text": "Constructs ",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": "have an "
        },
        {
          "text": "intelligence ",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": "attribute which determines "
        },
        {
          "text": "how many instructions they can follow in a lodestar",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": " (default is 8, with the exception of Wickerwood which is 5). \n\nWhen following a Delegation Station or Lodestar, if the number of "
        },
        {
          "text": "commands they have executed",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": " becomes higher than their intelligence, they will become confused and start the logic flow over.\n\nI can craft "
        },
        {
          "text": "Smart Construct Heads",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": " out of different materials to get around this limitation. \n\nI can also use conditional logic to help with this, as the "
        },
        {
          "text": "limit is how many tasks they have actually done, not how many are in the Delegation Station/Lodestar in total",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "json": [
        {
          "text": "Certain tasks within the "
        },
        {
          "text": "Lodestar ",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": "have a "
        },
        {
          "text": "minimum intelligence requirement",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": ", such as "
        },
        {
          "text": "Adventure, Mine, or Craft",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": ". If a construct's intelligence is lower than this requirement, it will not be able to perform the task (it counts as failure for logic flow purposes). \n\n"
        },
        {
          "text": "The specific intelligence requirements for tasks are detailed in their respective codex entries.",
          "italic": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "json": [
        {
          "text": "The smarter a construct is, the more it is capable of doing in general.  For example, smarter constructs will attempt to run away if they are ranged attacking and something is too close.  They are also capable of recognizing if their owner is undead and about to light on fire, and will spray them with water to give the Soaked effect before they ignite."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}